WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.146
Now the exciting part: real data!

2
00:00:03.146 --> 00:00:08.620
In this lesson, you will learn to open an Excel file in R, and

3
00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:14.485
then look at it from different angles, so you truly understand it before any analysis.

4
00:00:14.485 --> 00:00:16.440
Let's open our first dataset.

5
00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.447
Our working directory is set.

6
00:00:20.447 --> 00:00:22.594
Now I write a note:

7
00:00:22.594 --> 00:00:25.601
opening an Excel file. The line is:

8
00:00:25.601 --> 00:00:30.324
Dataset, arrow, read underscore excel, and the file name in quotes:

9
00:00:30.324 --> 00:00:33.760
Clean underscore Dataset dot x l s x.

10
00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:35.016
Run it.

11
00:00:35.016 --> 00:00:38.441
In the Environment pane, a new item appears:

12
00:00:38.441 --> 00:00:43.151
Dataset, with one hundred thirty five observations of twenty four variables.

13
00:00:43.151 --> 00:00:47.860
That means one hundred thirty five rows, and twenty four columns.

14
00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:50.928
Now let's look inside. First, head.

15
00:00:50.928 --> 00:00:54.929
Head shows the first six rows in the console:

16
00:00:54.929 --> 00:00:59.820
province, district, sex, marital status, household size, and the survey answers.

17
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.335
Next, str, for structure.

18
00:01:04.335 --> 00:01:07.021
It lists every column, and its type:

19
00:01:07.021 --> 00:01:09.707
chr for text, and num for numbers.

20
00:01:09.707 --> 00:01:15.080
This is the best way to check that each column has the right type.

21
00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:38.080
Unique shows the unique rows, and summary gives quick statistics for each column.

22
00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:58.820
For number columns, summary shows the minimum, the first quartile, the median, the mean,

23
00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:01.400
the third quartile, and the maximum.

24
00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:04.840
For household size, the average is about nineteen.

25
00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:30.980
Then colnames, which lists all the column names.

26
00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:37.340
Here we see the long survey question names, like, did you receive fifty kilograms of urea?

27
00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:56.789
And finally print, which prints the table in the console.

28
00:02:56.789 --> 00:03:00.380
I add a note above these lines: inspecting dataset.

29
00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:09.800
Head, str, summary, and colnames.

30
00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:13.000
Use them every time you open new data.

31
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:17.400
They are your first look, and your first check. Wonderful work!

